      <PackageReleaseNotes>v10.5.1 — No breaking changes. This release closes all six open GitHub issues (#11 gRPC, #13 NuGet GA, #14 tool use, #15 debate diff, #16 CLI, #18 cost gates) plus the two findings tracked only in docs/TASKS.

Correctness (W2-15): vector-store indexing is now idempotent. BaseRagProvider assigned Guid.NewGuid() as every point id, so re-indexing could never match an existing row and appended instead — three runs over 24 unique chunks left 72 points, diluting every later search. Both stores treat the id as an upsert key (Qdrant point id, pgvector ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE), so the id is now derived from the chunk's identity: (collection, source_path, chunk_index) when a source is known, (collection, content hash) when it is not. An edited chunk now overwrites its own point instead of orphaning the previous one.

⚠️ MIGRATION: collections written by 10.5.0 or earlier hold random-id points that re-indexing will never replace. Delete the collection through IVectorStore.DeleteCollectionAsync and re-index once. IndexFileAsync logs a warning when the pre-existing point count exceeds what the current document contributes, which is the signature of an unmigrated collection.

New — Function calling (#14): IToolProvider plus AIFunction tools. A member whose provider wraps a real IChatClient gets native function calling; any other provider gets the [[TOOL: name {json}]] marker protocol, because the string-only AsChatClient adapter ignores ChatOptions.Tools and flattens every message through message.Text, so function invocation on top of it is inert. Both paths write the same ToolCallLog, exposed on DebateRound.ToolCalls and DebateResult.ToolCalls. Ships FileSystemToolProvider (rooted, traversal-rejecting, size-capped), HttpToolProvider (host allow-list, refuses plain HTTP) and McpToolProvider.

New — Cost gates and rate limiting (#18): ICostGate, IRateLimiter, IModelPricingRegistry; BudgetCostGate, TokenBucketRateLimiter, ModelPricingRegistry, CostLedger. DebateResult.CostEstimate reports spend per member with an explicit IsEstimate flag. A denial does not throw: the debate returns a degraded result carrying what it already spent, because a ceiling that throws leaves the operator with no report. Configuring a ceiling without a price list logs a warning at construction — with no prices every call bills zero and the gate can never deny.

New — DebateResult.Diff (#15): DebateResultExtensions.Diff compares two runs, matching rounds by number and members by display name so a 4-round run against a 3-round run reports the missing round instead of shifting every later comparison. Word-level LCS rendered as **added** / ~~removed~~, plus Markdown and self-contained HTML export. TextSimilarity is now public and takes an explicit maximum length; the debate diff passes int.MaxValue so a verdict differing only in its last sentence cannot be reported as identical by inheriting the 1024-character stalemate cap.

New — delibera CLI (#16): run, resume, compare and benchmark on System.CommandLine. compare diffs two saved results; benchmark reports the distribution across runs rather than a single number.

New — gRPC transport (#11): Delibera.Grpc and Delibera.Grpc.Client, derived from the contracts the server already validates. The service layer sits on top of IDebateOrchestrator, so distributed execution and result caching behave identically over gRPC. Status ordinals are pinned in the proto because REST serialises the same states as strings.

Packaging (#13): Delibera.Server and Delibera.Redis are now publishable, so the GA matrix is Delibera.Core, Delibera.Server and Delibera.Redis. Server needed IsPackable=true — Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Web sets it false — plus its own README and icon, which must sit next to the csproj for pack to resolve them. Delibera.Grpc and Delibera.Grpc.Client carry the same metadata and are one workflow line away from publishing.

Tests: 595 unit tests, 595 passing (477 Core + 108 Server + 10 gRPC contract), 0 failed, 0 skipped. The release build is clean under -warnaserror. The 10.5.0 page for Delibera.Core states 514 tests (406 Core + 108 Server); that figure was measured at 10.4.0 and is corrected here.

Carried forward, still not fixed: W2-13 (WithCache missing from ICouncilBuilder) and W2-14 (cache-hit TotalDuration reports provenance, not caller wait time) are documented product decisions; pruning orphaned vector points when a source document shrinks needs a filter-delete on IVectorStore, which would be a breaking interface change; W4-10's bounded round channels remain open — the SSE writer itself is fixed, but Channel.CreateUnbounded is still in use in LocalDebateOrchestrator and RedisDebateOrchestrator, so a consumer that stops draining grows memory without limit.

v10.4.0 — Async Round Callback. Contains a BREAKING API CHANGE.

⚠️ BREAKING CHANGE (W3-07): IDebateStrategy.ExecuteAsync now takes Func&lt;DebateRound, CancellationToken, ValueTask&gt;? instead of Action&lt;DebateRound&gt;? for onRoundCompleted. Custom strategy implementations must change their parameter type and await the invocation. The public CouncilExecutor.OnRoundCompleted EVENT is unchanged and stays a fire-and-forget Action, so all existing event subscribers keep working. Migration: Action&lt;DebateRound&gt; r =&gt; { ... } becomes async (r, ct) =&gt; { ... } and onRoundCompleted?.Invoke(round) becomes if (onRoundCompleted is not null) await onRoundCompleted(round, ct).ConfigureAwait(false);

Why: the synchronous callback forced the executor to block on asynchronous work — one thread-pool thread pinned per debate for the duration of a file/network checkpoint write, on every round — and repackaged OperationCanceledException as AggregateException. The callback is now awaited, so no pool thread is held and cancellation propagates unchanged. See CHANGELOG for before/after code.

Performance: three allocation hot spots fixed. DebateCacheKeyGenerator no longer materializes the whole knowledge base on the heap to UTF-8 encode it — the buffer is ArrayPool-rented with an exact byte count, matching the pattern already proven in CompressionCache. TextSimilarity Levenshtein no longer allocates two int[n+1] rows per call: rows past 128 characters come from ArrayPool&lt;int&gt;, so the per-round response-similarity loop stops producing ~8 KiB of Gen0 garbage per comparison. Its doc comment claimed pooling that the code did not implement; it now matches the code.

Correctness: ModelContextWindowRegistry lookups were scanning a FrozenDictionary that was never probed by key, so the v10.3.0 "O(1) lookups" claim did not hold — and which pattern matched depended on dictionary enumeration order. A tagged name like "llama3.2:7b" matches both "llama3.2" (131072) and "llama3" (8192); correctness now rests on an explicit rule instead of authoring order: the exact pattern wins via a FrozenDictionary fast path, otherwise the longest matching substring wins via a pattern-ordered index with early exit. FrozenDictionary is now built with StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase explicitly — the parameterless ToFrozenDictionary() had silently fallen back to the ordinal default. 25 new tests pin the contract.

SSE streaming (SseDebateStreamWriter): two defects in the heartbeat pump made the stream wrong rather than slow. ValueTask.AsTask() returns a different Task instance on a second call, so the winner comparison was false even when an event had already arrived — every genuine event was misread as a heartbeat and skipped, and a debate streamed ZERO rounds. And after a heartbeat pulse the loop re-issued MoveNextAsync while the previous move was still in flight, then left that move pending when the method returned on a terminal event; an async iterator forbids both a concurrent move and a DisposeAsync with one in flight, so a debate completing mid-stream raised NotSupportedException AFTER its terminal event had already been written to the client. One MoveNextAsync is now created per iteration and reused across pulses, the winner is compared against that single task by reference, and any pending move is settled before disposal. Terminal events are still delivered exactly once, keep-alive comments are still emitted, and the reconnect hint is still first on the wire.

Dependencies: Microsoft.OpenApi is pinned to 2.12.2 in Delibera.Server. Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.12 still emits Example = ... assignments against IOpenApiMediaType.Example, which is read-only in Microsoft.OpenApi 3.x, so 3.10.2 fails the build with CS0200 inside generated OpenApiXmlCommentSupport code. 2.12.2 is the last published 2.x and the floor Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.12 requires ([2.12.0, 3.0.0)). Qdrant.Client 1.19.0 marks IVectorStore's QdrantClient.SearchAsync obsolete in favour of QueryAsync; QdrantVectorStore now calls QueryAsync with the same collection, query vector, limit, score threshold and cancellation token. The float[] -> VectorInput -> Query conversion is written as two statements because C# will not chain two user-defined implicit conversions.

Tests: 514 unit tests discovered, 514 passing (406 Core + 108 Server), 0 failed, 0 skipped. The five SSE failures carried since 10.3.x are fixed; the release build is clean under -warnaserror and a forced restore reports no NU1605/NU1608/NU1701.

v10.3.1 — Stabilization Pass. No new features and no breaking API changes: this is a correctness, performance and server-hardening pass over v10.3.0, plus the toolchain fixes needed to make a Release build and test clean. 489 tests discovered, 484 passing (381 Core + 103 Server), 0 skipped, 5 known SSE failures outstanding.

Correctness: SSE debate streaming delivers every round exactly once (draining record.Rounds alongside the orchestrator stream had sent earlier rounds twice), keeps the connection warm with a periodic comment, and now emits an explicit terminal outcome event instead of stopping silently; tenant isolation enforced across the debate endpoints; UseInMemoryCache no longer throws at resolve; the Redis debate worker no longer busy-loops on an idle stream.

Performance: TokenCounter rewritten (single-row Levenshtein over Span&lt;int&gt; + stackalloc, narrower lock scope), parallel debate execution, and DebateCacheKeyGenerator no longer hashes the knowledge base twice per debate.

Server hardening: request-validation filter plus FluentValidation validators for the scenario and corpus endpoints, RFC 7807 ProblemDetails exception handling, and concurrent-safety fixes in CorpusService.

Toolchain &amp; CI: NuGet versions aligned across the test projects (NU1605), Microsoft.SourceLink.Git bumped past CVE-2026-62900, and publish-nuget.yml builds the root solution so the test projects cannot be silently skipped.

v10.3.0 — Platform Release. Distributed debates (S-01), result caching (S-03), breaking-change cleanup (P-01), Delibera.Server Minimal API, and a comprehensive performance &amp; integrity pass.

⚠️ BREAKING CHANGES (P-01) — upgrade from v10.2.x: Moderator → Chairman (all APIs); IDebateStrategyWithOptions removed (use IDebateStrategy with DebateExecutionOptions); ModelCapabilities now non-nullable (use ModelCapabilities.IsUnknown); RagProviderFactory → VectorStoreFactory / IRagProviderFactory → IVectorStoreFactory; ILLMProvider.GetModelCapabilitiesAsync must return ModelCapabilities.Unknown(model) when unknown; DebateStatus.Paused and DebateOrchestrationStatus.Pending removed (dead enums); WeightedVotingStrategy.ResolveWeight now falls back to ballot.Weight (per-ballot weights work as documented); DebateRecord.Label defaults to string.Empty; DebateResponse now includes Label/CacheHit/CacheKey; SseDebateStreamWriter now consumes IDebateOrchestrator.StreamAsync (was DebateRecord.RoundReader); DebateOrchestrationService rewritten from 200ms polling to event-driven streaming.

S-01 Distributed Debates: IDebateOrchestrator interface (ExecuteAsync/EnqueueAsync/GetStatusAsync/StreamAsync/CancelAsync) abstracts debate execution from transport; LocalDebateOrchestrator (default in-process); RedisDebateOrchestrator (Delibera.Redis project) publishing round events to Redis Streams; DebateHandle/DebateOrchestrationStatus/DebateRoundEvent; DebateWorkerService background consumer; ICouncilBuilder.WithOrchestrator fluent API.

S-03 Result Caching: IDebateCache interface (GetAsync/SetAsync/InvalidateAsync/ExistsAsync); CacheBehavior enum (Disabled/ReadWrite/ReadOnly/WriteThrough/Bypass); DebateCacheKeyGenerator (deterministic SHA-256 key); InMemoryDebateCache/FileDebateCache/RedisDebateCache implementations; DebateResult.CacheHit/CacheKey/CachedAt; ICouncilBuilder.WithCacheBehavior and WithCache; DI extensions UseInMemoryCache/UseFileCache/UseRedisCache; debate.cache_hit OpenTelemetry counter.

Performance &amp; Integrity: 30-min Timer-based eviction on LocalDebateOrchestrator/DebateOrchestrationService/RedisDebateOrchestrator (prevents unbounded growth); ProviderFactory disposed via using var in all server templates and ScenarioBuilder; CompressionCache and FileDebateStore now implement IDisposable; volatile int backing field for DebateRecord.Status and DebateEntry.Status (cross-thread visibility); ProviderFactory.CachingFactory uses ConcurrentDictionary+GetOrAdd; ConfigureAwait(false) added to all await in Delibera.Core and Delibera.Redis; fire-and-forget CancelAsync wrapped in Task.Run with try/catch; IDebateCache/IDebateStore/IDebateOrchestrator.GetStatusAsync return ValueTask (no Task allocation on sync paths); FrozenDictionary/FrozenSet in ModelContextWindowRegistry (O(1) lookups, cache invalidated on Register); JsonSerializer.SerializeToUtf8Bytes in SseDebateStreamWriter and DebateCacheKeyGenerator; single-row Levenshtein with Span&lt;int&gt;+stackalloc; ReaderWriterLockSlim in TokenCounter; readonly record struct RankedOption; StringBuilder capacity hints in DebateResult.ToMarkdown.

514 unit tests discovered, 509 passing (406 Core + 103 Server), 5 SSE failures outstanding. v10.2.7 — Multi-Modal Council (F-06). v10.2.6 — Nine new features: streaming, voting, persistence, agent memory, observability, structured output, adaptive strategy, templates, quick wins. v10.2.5 — OllamaProvider maxOutputTokens fix. v10.2.4 — Full CancellationToken support. v10.2.0–v10.2.3 — AutoChunking, Polly v8, parallel Operator, response language, logging, M.E.AI bridge.</PackageReleaseNotes>
